// ── GRAMMAR (extended) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const g2 = [
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is a gerund? Give examples.','A GERUND is a verb form ending in "-ing" that functions as a NOUN.\nExamples:\n• "Swimming is good exercise." (subject)\n• "She enjoys reading." (object)\n• "His hobby is cooking." (predicate noun)\n\nDo NOT confuse with present participle (also -ing but functions as adjective or in continuous tense):\n• "The running water…" (participle — adjective)\n• "She is running." (present participle in continuous)'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is an infinitive? How is it used?','An INFINITIVE is "to + base verb" and can function as noun, adjective, or adverb.\nAs NOUN: "To succeed requires hard work." (subject)\nAs ADJECTIVE: "She has a goal to achieve." (modifies "goal")\nAs ADVERB: "He studies hard to pass." (why he studies)\n\nSplit infinitive: Placing an adverb between "to" and the verb. ("to boldly go") — acceptable in modern English but avoided in formal writing.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What are the four types of sentences by purpose?','1. DECLARATIVE: Makes a statement. Ends with a period.\n   "The sun rises in the east."\n2. INTERROGATIVE: Asks a question. Ends with a question mark.\n   "What time does the exam start?"\n3. IMPERATIVE: Gives a command or request. Usually ends with period.\n   "Study your lessons every day."\n4. EXCLAMATORY: Expresses strong emotion. Ends with exclamation point.\n   "What a beautiful day!"'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What are the four types of sentences by structure?','1. SIMPLE: One independent clause.\n   "She passed the exam."\n2. COMPOUND: Two or more independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction.\n   "She studied hard, and she passed the exam."\n3. COMPLEX: One independent clause + one or more dependent clauses.\n   "Because she studied hard, she passed the exam."\n4. COMPOUND-COMPLEX: Two independent + at least one dependent clause.\n   "She studied hard, and because of this, she passed the exam."'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between "affect" and "effect"?','"AFFECT" is usually a VERB meaning to have an influence on.\n"EFFECT" is usually a NOUN meaning a result or outcome.\n\nExamples:\n• "Stress can AFFECT your health." (verb)\n• "The EFFECT of stress on health is well-documented." (noun)\n\nMemory trick: AFFECT = Action (verb) | EFFECT = End result (noun)\n\nException: "Effect" can be a verb meaning to bring about: "The new policy will EFFECT change."'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between "lay" and "lie"?','"LAY" requires an object (transitive): to put something down.\n• Present: lay | Past: laid | Past participle: laid\n• "Please LAY the book on the table."\n\n"LIE" does NOT take an object (intransitive): to recline or be in a flat position.\n• Present: lie | Past: lay | Past participle: lain\n• "I need to LIE down."\n• "Yesterday, he LAY on the couch all day."\n\nTip: Chickens lay eggs (lay = put something). People lie down (lie = recline).'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between "its" and "it\'s"?','"ITS" (no apostrophe) is a possessive pronoun. It shows ownership.\n• "The dog wagged ITS tail."\n• "The company lost ITS license."\n\n"IT\'S" (with apostrophe) is a contraction of "it is" or "it has."\n• "IT\'S raining outside." (It is raining)\n• "IT\'S been a long day." (It has been)\n\nTest: If you can replace it with "it is" or "it has" → use IT\'S. Otherwise → use ITS.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between "their," "there," and "they\'re"?','"THEIR" = possessive pronoun (belonging to them)\n• "The students submitted THEIR papers."\n\n"THERE" = refers to a place, or introduces a sentence\n• "The library is THERE on the left."\n• "THERE are many students who passed."\n\n"THEY\'RE" = contraction of "they are"\n• "THEY\'RE studying for the board exam."\n\nMemory: THEIR has "heir" (possession). THERE has "here" (place). THEY\'RE = they are.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the correct use of "who" vs. "that" for people?','"WHO" is used for people (and sometimes animals with names).\n"THAT" is used for things, ideas, and sometimes people.\n\nPreferred usage:\n• "The student WHO topped the exam..." (people → WHO preferred)\n• "The book THAT I read..." (thing → THAT)\n• "The team THAT won..." (group → THAT acceptable)\n\nNote: Using "that" for people is grammatically acceptable but "who" is stylistically preferred in formal writing.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between "principal" and "principle"?','"PRINCIPAL" (noun/adjective): The head of a school; main; chief; a sum of money.\n• "The school PRINCIPAL called a meeting."\n• "The PRINCIPAL cause of the problem was negligence."\n• "The loan PRINCIPAL is ₱50,000."\n\n"PRINCIPLE" (noun only): A fundamental truth, rule, or belief.\n• "The company operates on the PRINCIPLE of integrity."\n• "The PRINCIPLES of democracy include equality and freedom."\n\nMemory: princiPAL — your PAL (the school head). princiPLE — a ruLE.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the correct form? "The team (has/have) won the championship."','In American English: "The team HAS won the championship."\nCollective nouns (team, committee, jury, class, staff) are treated as SINGULAR in American English.\n\nIn British English, collective nouns may take plural verbs: "The team HAVE won."\n\nIn Philippine CSE exams, the American English convention is generally used:\n• "The jury HAS reached a verdict."\n• "The committee HAS approved the proposal."'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is a coordinating conjunction vs. a subordinating conjunction?','COORDINATING conjunctions join EQUAL elements (FANBOYS: For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So).\n• Join two independent clauses: "I studied, BUT I failed."\n\nSUBORDINATING conjunctions join a DEPENDENT clause to an INDEPENDENT clause:\nCommon: although, because, since, when, while, if, unless, until, after, before, as\n• "ALTHOUGH I studied hard, I failed."\n• "BECAUSE she was sick, she missed the exam."\n\nRule: A comma is used when the dependent clause COMES FIRST.'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'Identify the part of speech: "The very tired students barely noticed the bell."','VERY — adverb (modifies the adjective "tired")\nTIRED — adjective (describes "students")\nSTUDENTS — noun (subject)\nBARELY — adverb (modifies "noticed")\nNOTICED — verb (past tense)\nTHE — definite article (adjective modifying "students" and "bell")\nBELL — noun (direct object)'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the difference between a direct and indirect object?','DIRECT OBJECT: receives the action directly; answers "what?" or "whom?"\n• "She gave a book." (gave what? → book = direct object)\n\nINDIRECT OBJECT: indicates TO WHOM or FOR WHOM the action is done; answers "to whom?" or "for whom?"\n• "She gave MARIA a book." (gave to whom? → Maria = indirect object)\n\nFormula: Subject + Verb + [Indirect Object] + Direct Object\n• "The teacher gave the STUDENTS their grades."'),
  f(T.PRO_grammar,'What is the passive voice formula and when should it be used?','Formula: Subject + BE (correct tense) + Past Participle\nExamples:\n• Active: "The principal signs the letter." → Passive: "The letter IS SIGNED by the principal."\n• Active: "They built the bridge." → Passive: "The bridge WAS BUILT."\n\nUse passive voice when:\n1. The doer is UNKNOWN: "The window was broken."\n2. The doer is UNIMPORTANT: "The report was submitted on time."\n3. To EMPHASIZE the receiver: "The president was assassinated."'),
  f(T.SUB_grammar,'What is a noun? Give examples of types.','A NOUN is a word that names a person, place, thing, or idea.\n\nTypes:\n• COMMON: general names (teacher, city, book)\n• PROPER: specific names, capitalized (Maria, Manila, Bible)\n• CONCRETE: can be perceived by the senses (table, water, music)\n• ABSTRACT: ideas or concepts (love, justice, freedom)\n• COLLECTIVE: groups (team, class, committee, flock)\n• COUNT: can be counted (book, apple, student)\n• MASS/UNCOUNTABLE: cannot be counted individually (water, rice, advice)'),
  f(T.SUB_grammar,'What are the common irregular verbs in English?','Common irregular verb forms:\n• go — went — gone\n• come — came — come\n• see — saw — seen\n• eat — ate — eaten\n• write — wrote — written\n• take — took — taken\n• give — gave — given\n• know — knew — known\n• speak — spoke — spoken\n• think — thought — thought\n• buy — bought — bought\n• bring — brought — brought\n• teach — taught — taught\n• break — broke — broken'),
  f(T.SUB_grammar,'What are the tenses of verbs? (Simple tenses only)','Three simple tenses:\n\nSIMPLE PRESENT: habitual action or general truth.\n• "She studies every day."\n\nSIMPLE PAST: completed action in the past.\n• "She studied yesterday."\n\nSIMPLE FUTURE: action that will happen.\n• "She will study tomorrow."\n\nFor irregular verbs, the past form changes (go→went).\nFor regular verbs, add "-ed" for past (study→studied, walk→walked).'),
  f(T.SUB_grammar,'What is subject-verb agreement with compound subjects?','COMPOUND subjects joined by AND → PLURAL verb.\n• "Maria AND Juan ARE late."\n\nCompound subjects joined by OR/NOR → verb agrees with the CLOSER subject.\n• "Either the teachers OR the principal IS responsible."\n• "Either the principal OR the teachers ARE responsible."\n\nException: Mathematical operations join with AND but take SINGULAR.\n• "Two plus two IS four."'),
  f(T.SUB_grammar,'What is the difference between present perfect and simple past?','SIMPLE PAST: action completed at a specific time in the past.\n• "I ate lunch at noon." (specific time)\n• "She graduated in 2020." (specific year)\n\nPRESENT PERFECT: action completed at an unspecified time OR action that connects past to present.\n• "I have eaten." (no specific time)\n• "She has graduated." (result affects present)\n• "I have lived here for 10 years." (started in past, still true)\n\nSignal words for present perfect: already, yet, just, ever, never, for, since, recently'),
];

